- I am thinking through how to translate 17th century fur trade accounts into something digital. 
- Important info for someone who is not me: I don't know anything about accounting. I am bad at spreadsheets. I read these account books so that I can learn (often qualitative) stuff about material culture (shirts mostly). Through reading them a bunch, I have figured out how the numbers fit together just so I can understand what they mean so I don't accidentally say "Indigenous people bought 30 blue shirts that year," when in reality 20 of those shirts were actually purchased by employees, 5 were eaten by rats, and only 5 were purchased in the fur trade.
- The yearly account books are essentially relational databases. They are series of linked tables. The way they are linked is through the accountants brain.
	- often the accountant writes "by my other books" to note where the link is

### Mindmapping the links and tables
- I understand the links in an intuitive way after just reading these things a bunch, but those links can also be expressed in a bleep bloop math computer way
- To start to understand this I made a mindmap illustrating how they are linked. **I am not yet actually showing what rows and columns on one page go into what rows and columns on the other, instead I'm just showing what pages (tables) are actually linked**
- I played around with obsidian's Canvas feature to do this. Usually I just draw stuff like this out by hand, but I did this so it would just be on my computer. I haven't played with this feature before because I think the whole point of obsidian is keeping stuff texty, but this was pretty useful. 
	- It was nice dragging stuff around. For example, after writing out every page and how they're connected, that helped me more clearly conceptualize where the actual data inputs were. The account does totals and ledgers based on tallies that are done at the beginning of the book. I knew this, but its nice putting all those weird pages together in one coloured box and definitively calling them the same class of thing.

## My mind map
- The HBC used standardized double entry bookkeeping, but they had some quirks with how they did stuff.
	- They didn't record individual transactions, because trade happened in large volumes. Lots of bulk, expenses, bartering, gifts, feasts, etc happened around a few weeks of summer trade, and barely anything happened for the rest of the year.
	- Because of this, they just compared what was missing at the end of the year with what was gained
		- I have no record of them counting inventory or keeping a tally of what trade goods went out, so one of the data inputs (trade goods out) is a bit of a mystery as to how it was collected.
			- The ledger means that they could have either figured out how many items were missing through current inventory count OR the other way around.
				- same with furs i guess, except they started the year with zero furs so that's simpler.
