## Conclusions/ thoughts
- What this process helped me realize is that if I wanted to create a digital version of the account books, I could effectively do this without OCRing every page. Everything else can be inferred just through the "counts", just like how the 17th century accountant derived the rest of the books through the counts. (men's debts, )
	- This wouldn't capture quirks and mistakes, but that's probably okay for most people's uses. (I don't have uses in mind right now)
- This is interesting, because I rarely look at these counts. I look at the ledger. But that's because the ledger is essentially doing some data analysis stuff. It's useful to me because it shows me all the info in the counts at a glance, but filtered by item. Obviously, that was useful to HBC executives too, since they got someone to put all that work in. (I mean, theres an error-mitigating purpose of the ledger too, but since the accounts were all sent to london imagine there was something useful in being able to see all that, rather than just the overplus.)
- Why not a graph database?
	- There is a lot of qualitative data in the "expenses" section. I kind of think a graph database might be better suited to analyzing that, but I think you would need the hard numbers side of things to make sense of it anyway. 
	- The account books already work like a relational database, so this would just be creating a digital version of what is there. Then, if you want to extract bits of it to put into a graph database, you could.
	- I still don't really understand what a graph database is that well. And I'm not sure if networky stuff with linked spreadsheets is impossible or just requires more steps.
- I am not sure what my project is going to be. I am talking as though I were doing a huge digitization project, not because that's what I'm actually going to do. I think the experiment is maybe going to be doing some tests to figure out how one might approach a project like that.


